{
  "concepts": [
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "This member's traversal is a typed contract with id, domain, codomain, context, owner, scope, basis, preconditions, postconditions, authority, evidence, provenance and stop_states.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":926,
          "quote":"v = {\n  id,\n  domain,\n  codomain,\n  context,\n  owner,\n  scope,\n  basis,\n  preconditions,\n  postconditions,\n  authority,\n  evidence,\n  provenance,\n  stop_states\n}",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS%2F5.%20Traversals%20and%20functor%20kinds",
          "start_line":912
        }
      ],
      "key":"traversal_contract",
      "label":"Traversal contract"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "F_D, urn:stdo:concept:axiomatic-calculus:f-d, classifies deterministic evaluation or proof over declared properties. It returns a judgment without performing the domain construction judged.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":937,
          "quote":"F_D = urn:stdo:concept:axiomatic-calculus:f-d",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS%2F5.%20Traversals%20and%20functor%20kinds",
          "start_line":937
        },
        {
          "end_line":975,
          "quote":"- `F_D` classifies deterministic evaluation or proof over declared\n  properties. It returns a judgment and does not perform the domain\n  construction being judged (P6).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS%2F5.%20Traversals%20and%20functor%20kinds",
          "start_line":973
        }
      ],
      "key":"deterministic_functor",
      "label":"F_D"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "F_P, urn:stdo:concept:axiomatic-calculus:f-p, classifies bounded probabilistic interpretation, construction or proposal under an exact traversal contract. Usefulness or repeatability confers no semantic, admission, acceptance or closure authority on its result.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":938,
          "quote":"F_P = urn:stdo:concept:axiomatic-calculus:f-p",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS%2F5.%20Traversals%20and%20functor%20kinds",
          "start_line":938
        },
        {
          "end_line":979,
          "quote":"- `F_P` classifies bounded probabilistic interpretation, construction or\n  proposal under an exact traversal contract. Its result has no semantic,\n  admission, acceptance or closure authority merely because it is useful\n  or repeatable (P6, P0; instance premise (iii) of §1 Foundation to law).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS%2F5.%20Traversals%20and%20functor%20kinds",
          "start_line":976
        }
      ],
      "key":"probabilistic_functor",
      "label":"F_P"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "F_H, urn:stdo:concept:axiomatic-calculus:f-h, classifies explicit human adjudication under exact identity, authority grant, subject, basis and decision contract. Human presence grants no ambient authority.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":939,
          "quote":"F_H = urn:stdo:concept:axiomatic-calculus:f-h",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS%2F5.%20Traversals%20and%20functor%20kinds",
          "start_line":939
        },
        {
          "end_line":982,
          "quote":"- `F_H` classifies explicit human adjudication under an exact identity,\n  authority grant, subject, basis and decision contract. Human presence\n  grants no ambient authority (P6).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS%2F5.%20Traversals%20and%20functor%20kinds",
          "start_line":980
        }
      ],
      "key":"human_functor",
      "label":"F_H"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "F_K[v](X_v) -> Y_v | Omega_v denotes one functor kind applied under one exact traversal identity to its typed input domain, yielding its typed result domain or a value of its closed stop algebra. Omega_v refines the core result algebra without adding a core value.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":952,
          "quote":"where `F_K` is one functor kind, `v` is one exact traversal identity,\n`X_v` and `Y_v` are its typed input and result domains, and `Omega_v` is\nits closed stop algebra. `Omega_v` is a refinement of the core result\nalgebra of [FOUNDATIONS §4 Results](FOUNDATIONS.md#results): it adds no\ncore value",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS%2F5.%20Traversals%20and%20functor%20kinds",
          "start_line":948
        }
      ],
      "key":"functor_application",
      "label":"Functor application and stop domain"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Typed composition orders a first traversal v and a second traversal w, written F_K2[w] compose F_K1[v], while retaining their separate contracts and results.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1018,
          "quote":"Composition retains both traversal identities, intermediate results,\njudgments, evidence, authority, provenance and stop states.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS%2F5.%20Traversals%20and%20functor%20kinds%2FAC-016%20Typed%20composition",
          "start_line":1017
        }
      ],
      "key":"typed_composition",
      "label":"Typed composition"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Interpret_a_c(subject_basis, signature, interpretation_contract) applies a_c to one exact subject and yields a proposed M_subject* or hold, gap or refusal. The star denotes absence of the applicable semantic acceptance judgment; stops take their cause-dependent core values under AC-007.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1038,
          "quote":"Interpret_a_c(subject_basis, signature, interpretation_contract)\n  -> M_subject* | hold | gap | refusal\n```\n\nThe stops `hold`, `gap` and `refusal` take their core values under AC-007\nby their causes. The trailing `*` marks a proposed model that has not\nreceived the\napplicable semantic acceptance judgment.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS%2F6.%20Interpretation%20and%20models",
          "start_line":1029
        }
      ],
      "key":"subject_interpretation",
      "label":"Exact subject interpretation"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Structural conformance covers closed syntax, types, identities, references, bases and mechanically decidable predicates. Semantic fidelity covers interpretation meaning and materiality. Semantic acceptance is a separately recorded authority judgment.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1075,
          "quote":"structural conformance of a model covers its closed syntax, types,\nidentities, references, bases and mechanically decidable predicates, and\nthe semantic fidelity of an interpretation covers its meaning and\nmateriality (P1, P6).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS%2F6.%20Interpretation%20and%20models%2FAC-018%20Structural%20and%20semantic%20separation",
          "start_line":1072
        },
        {
          "end_line":1056,
          "quote":"Semantic acceptance remains a separately recorded authority judgment (P6,\nP1).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS%2F6.%20Interpretation%20and%20models",
          "start_line":1055
        }
      ],
      "key":"model_assessment",
      "label":"Structural, semantic and acceptance assessments"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "M_b satisfies a_c[Sigma] names a valid-model claim under an exact signature and calculus basis, subject to AC-019's necessary conditions.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1080,
          "quote":"A model `M_b` is a valid model of `a_c` under signature `Sigma`, written\n`M_b satisfies a_c[Sigma]`, only when (P6, P1):",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS%2F6.%20Interpretation%20and%20models%2FAC-019%20Valid%20model",
          "start_line":1079
        }
      ],
      "key":"valid_model_claim",
      "label":"Valid model claim"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "a_c is the pure calculus layer.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1102,
          "quote":"a_c       = the pure calculus",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS%2F7.%20Layers",
          "start_line":1102
        }
      ],
      "key":"pure_calculus",
      "label":"a_c"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "a_c.X is subject X interpreted as a model of a_c. Acceptance is external to its unchanged content identity; an accepted interpretation is the pair (a_c.X, J_X).",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1103,
          "quote":"a_c.X     = subject X interpreted as a model of a_c",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS%2F7.%20Layers",
          "start_line":1103
        },
        {
          "end_line":1227,
          "quote":"The accepted\ninterpretation is the pair `(a_c.X, J_X)` where `J_X` is the applicable\naccepted judgment.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS%2F8.%20Calculus%20identity",
          "start_line":1225
        }
      ],
      "key":"interpreted_subject_layer",
      "label":"a_c.X"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "a_c.X.C is the accepted model encoded in carrier C. G_C denotes encoded bytes; J_X is external semantic acceptance over unchanged M_X; D_C is deterministic carrier admission under v_carrier_admission using G_C, Profile_C and CarrierBasis_C, over unchanged carrier bytes. The admitted encoding evidence is the exact triple (G_C, J_X, D_C).",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1114,
          "quote":"M_X = Interpret_a_c(X)\nJ_X = Accept_X(M_X) -> accepted | refuse\nG_C = Encode_C(M_X, J_X) where J_X = accepted\nD_C = F_D[v_carrier_admission](G_C, Profile_C, CarrierBasis_C)\n      -> admitted | refuse",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS%2F7.%20Layers",
          "start_line":1110
        },
        {
          "end_line":1123,
          "quote":"`D_C` is a judgment over unchanged carrier bytes; it is not a\ntransformed carrier. The admitted encoding evidence relation is the exact\ntriple `(G_C, J_X, D_C)`; neither judgment enters the identity of its\nsubject (P6, P3).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS%2F7.%20Layers",
          "start_line":1120
        }
      ],
      "key":"encoded_carrier_layer",
      "label":"a_c.X.C and encoding evidence"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "AxiomaticCalculusBasis is an external, post-publication record distinguishing the stable calculus concept from an immutable edition. It binds governing premise clauses through derivation_basis and the published member through publication_basis within one exact immutable package.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1155,
          "quote":"The stable concept identity does not identify one immutable calculus\nedition. An exact released calculus basis is an external record created\nafter the publication bytes exist. It binds the governing premise clauses\nand the published calculus member as distinct roles inside one exact\nimmutable package (P3, P6):",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS%2F8.%20Calculus%20identity",
          "start_line":1151
        }
      ],
      "key":"calculus_basis_record",
      "label":"AxiomaticCalculusBasis"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "An application binding is an external typed import, translation or specialisation of an a_c concept declared under the application's own exact authority, context, basis, scope, preservation, loss, refusal and invalidation law.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1277,
          "quote":"An application may declare a typed import, translation or specialisation\nof an `a_c` concept under that application's own exact authority, context,\nbasis, scope, preservation, loss, refusal and invalidation law. Such a\nrelation is external to the calculus.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS%2F9.%20Application%20boundary%2C%20imports%20and%20exclusions%2FExternal%20imports%20and%20specialisations",
          "start_line":1274
        }
      ],
      "key":"application_binding",
      "label":"External application binding"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A constraint surface is a derived task view specifying a declared subject and work boundary's objects and meanings, predicates and dependencies, admissible operations, required results, authority, evidence, permitted freedom and unresolved conditions. It bounds work without prescribing internal strategy beyond those obligations and supports recovery of task constraints within finite attention.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1289,
          "quote":"A constraint surface states, for a declared subject and work boundary,\nthe obligations and the permitted freedom of the work: its objects and\ntheir meanings, the predicates and dependencies that bear on it, the\nadmissible operations, the required results, the authority, the evidence\nand the conditions still unresolved. It constrains the work without\nprescribing the worker's internal solution strategy beyond those\nobligations (P2, P6).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS%2F9.%20Application%20boundary%2C%20imports%20and%20exclusions%2FConstraint%20surfaces",
          "start_line":1283
        }
      ],
      "key":"constraint_surface",
      "label":"Constraint surface"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Calculus review and qualification evaluate explicitly bounded claims about the member, including its signature, laws, identities, references, conservation properties and absence of hidden application dependencies.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1386,
          "quote":"Review and qualification of this member cover at least (P6):\n\n1. closed signature, `RecordKind_ac`, population, field, value-domain and\n   reference-domain definitions;",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS%2F10.%20Conformance%20obligations",
          "start_line":1383
        }
      ],
      "key":"calculus_qualification",
      "label":"Calculus qualification"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "CONFORMANCE is a Tier 2 catalogue importing method rules for shared owners and consumers without defining a competing result algebra. A discriminator is a situation whose expected distinction separates a conforming application from a defective one. A case names subject, mechanism and expected distinction; a separate execution record binds exact tested subject, basis, acquired context, observations, assessor and result.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":13,
          "quote":"Tier 2. Defines conformance cases for the method's shared owners and their\nconsumers. A discriminator is a situation whose expected distinction\nseparates a conforming application from a defective one. Each expectation\nand its deciding local owner are stated here; this member imports the\nmethod's rules and defines no competing result algebra.\n\nA case names its subject, mechanism and expected distinction. An execution\nrecord separately binds the exact tested subject, basis, acquired context,\nobservations, assessor and result.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/CONFORMANCE.md#CONFORMANCE",
          "start_line":3
        }
      ],
      "key":"conformance_catalogue",
      "label":"Conformance catalogue and cases"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The named core values are satisfied, falsified, indeterminate, out_of_frame and invalid_basis. CONFORMANCE imports them from FOUNDATIONS. Its re_enter, re_entry_requested(authority), not_effected and completion_unknown are ACTIVATION-defined refinements mapped into that core. Non-performance has no result.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":31,
          "quote":"Result values. The values `satisfied`, `falsified`, `indeterminate`,\n`out_of_frame` and `invalid_basis` named in this member are core values of\n[FOUNDATIONS §4 Results](FOUNDATIONS.md#results). The values `re_enter`,\n`re_entry_requested(authority)`, `not_effected` and `completion_unknown`\nare refinements that\n[ACTIVATION §3](ACTIVATION.md#3-step-results-and-disposition) defines and\nmaps into that core. This member defines no result value\n(P5, P6).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/CONFORMANCE.md#CONFORMANCE",
          "start_line":24
        }
      ],
      "key":"conformance_result_domains",
      "label":"Imported conformance result domains"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Counterexample cases C-01 through C-12 identify failure mechanisms and local rules expected to distinguish them; they are assessment designs rather than reports of observed failures or passes.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":36,
          "quote":"These cases identify failure mechanisms and the local rules that must\ndistinguish them.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/CONFORMANCE.md#CONFORMANCE%2F1.%20Counterexample%20cases",
          "start_line":35
        }
      ],
      "key":"counterexample_cases",
      "label":"Counterexample cases"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Construction trials T-01 through T-08 test bounded construction, ambiguity, composition, failure conservation, recovery, requirement change, defective acceptance and broader sourced decision work.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":57,
          "quote":"| T-01 Context cap and decomposition | required basis exceeds one actor's declared cap; repeat with tighter caps and a coupled case that cannot fit | refinement preserves the outer contract under independent assessment; the cannot-fit case returns out_of_frame or a lawful escalation, never omission |",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/CONFORMANCE.md#CONFORMANCE%2F2.%20Construction%20trials",
          "start_line":57
        }
      ],
      "key":"construction_trials",
      "label":"Construction trials"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "R-01 and R-02 test fresh-context recovery of governing relations from the corpus, including matched comparisons when claimed and operation without validator folklore, author memory or whole-corpus loading.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":79,
          "quote":"| R-02 No private knowledge | the same with validator folklore, author memory and whole-corpus loading withheld | the activation still succeeds or returns out_of_frame truthfully |",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/CONFORMANCE.md#CONFORMANCE%2F3.%20Recoverability",
          "start_line":79
        }
      ],
      "key":"recoverability_cases",
      "label":"Recoverability cases"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "CD construction-discriminator cases exercise configurations, executor bindings and workspace recovery through actual use. AR assurance-relation cases exercise weak assertions, insufficient observations, shared error and change selection.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":91,
          "quote":"A case here is\nnamed CD (construction discriminator); the numbers in brackets are those of the\ndiscriminators it exercises.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/CONFORMANCE.md#CONFORMANCE%2F4.%20Cases%20for%20method%20discriminators",
          "start_line":89
        },
        {
          "end_line":110,
          "quote":"The four cases below carry them; a case here is named AR (assurance\nrelation). Each applicable case requires its own exact execution and assessment\nrecord; no run or result is asserted by this catalogue.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/CONFORMANCE.md#CONFORMANCE%2F4.%20Cases%20for%20method%20discriminators",
          "start_line":108
        }
      ],
      "key":"method_use_assurance_cases",
      "label":"Construction-discriminator and assurance-relation cases"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Discriminator coverage associates each situation and expected distinction with local owning sections and checks. L-n denotes a qualified reviewer's reasoned textual review without execution; actual-use cases add observed evidence. J53–J59 concern integrated assessment across members and their material composition.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":129,
          "quote":"A logical-review check is a reviewer's reasoned reading: the reviewer\nconstructs a discriminator's situation against the text and finds whether\nthe text yields the expected distinction, or finds a counterexample or a\ncontradiction, without running anything (P6).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/CONFORMANCE.md#CONFORMANCE%2F5.%20Discriminator%20coverage",
          "start_line":121
        },
        {
          "end_line":197,
          "quote":"Integrated assessment checks the complete declared member set and its\nmaterial composition. Local success cannot discharge a contradictory\nshared determination, an unresolved owner decision or a required result\ngap.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/CONFORMANCE.md#CONFORMANCE%2F5.%20Discriminator%20coverage",
          "start_line":194
        }
      ],
      "key":"discriminator_coverage",
      "label":"Logical and integrated discriminator coverage"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "UC-1–UC-9 are construction cases and AS-1–AS-12 are assurance cases with stated expected distinctions. Each receives local-owner logical review and applicable actual-use evidence where required by the claim. These cases remain assessment design.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":222,
          "quote":"The following nine construction cases and twelve assurance cases state\ntheir complete expected distinctions. UC-n identifies a construction case;\nAS-n identifies an assurance case. Each receives a logical-review check\nunder its local owner, and applicable actual-use evidence where the claim\nrequires it.",
