    {
      "conditions": ["Once the project has decided its outcomes and capabilities."],
      "definition": "The profile concerns realisation design and implementation shape. Its goals are reduced ambiguity, entropy, avoidable debt, hidden authority, hidden mutation and semantic drift; increased refactorability; and preserved evidence for audit and reconstruction. Bounded intermediate traversal gives finite inspectable units for reasoning, implementation, evaluation, proof and refactor; a module boundary is one such tool.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":91,
          "quote":"The goals are to reduce ambiguity, reduce\nentropy, eliminate avoidable technical debt, increase refactorability,\nreduce hidden authority, hidden mutation and semantic drift in realisation\ncode, and preserve enough evidence that later reviewers can audit,\nrefactor and reconstruct why the realisation took its current shape (P2,\nP6).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F1.%20Purpose%20and%20position%2FPurpose",
          "start_line":85
        },
        {
          "end_line":107,
          "quote":"Specification gives the global WHAT; every intermediate\ntraversal should have a finite, inspectable unit for reasoning,\nimplementation, evaluation, proof and later refactor. A module boundary is\none realisation-level tool for creating that unit; it is not the only\npossible bounded traversal.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F1.%20Purpose%20and%20position%2FPurpose",
